('1fc8203e-fc84-5ded-947d-1ad169ff55ca'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"[Figure: CP-c26-02 — scatterplot with line of best fit; x 0–12, y 36–48; points near (1,37), (2,41), (3,39), (4,43), (5,40), (6,39), (7,45), (8,48), (10,42), (11,48); line from ≈(0,38) to ≈(11,47)]\nThe scatterplot shows the relationship between two variables, x and y. A line of best fit is also shown. Which of the following is closest to the predicted value of y at x = 8.5?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"42.1"},{"key":"B","text":"43.9"},{"key":"C","text":"44.8"},{"key":"D","text":"45.3"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-02","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/CP-c26-02.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for CP-c26-02","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"C"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-02)."'::jsonb),
('161f58d5-7e9b-5d99-b909-0cb553187208'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'medium','grid_in','"[Figure: CP-c26-03 — scatterplot with line of best fit; x 0–7, y 0–14; points near (1,6), (2,2), (3,5), (4,8), (5,9), (6,12), (6,8), (7,8); line from ≈(0,3) to ≈(7,10.5)]\nThe scatterplot shows the relationship between two variables, x and y. A line of best fit is also shown. For how many of the data points does the line of best fit predict a y-value that is greater than the actual y-value?"'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-03","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/CP-c26-03.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for CP-c26-03","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"4"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-03)."'::jsonb),
('d9eb4ad7-bb1e-53ef-ac08-d6888d1863fa'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"A random sample of 400 people from a town were interviewed about their attitudes toward community wellness programs. Based on this sample, it is estimated that 58% of the town''s population supports additional funding for community wellness programs, with an associated margin of error of 6%. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion from these data?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"Exactly 58% of the town''s population supports additional funding for community wellness programs."},{"key":"B","text":"It is plausible that between 52% and 64% of the town''s population supports additional funding for community wellness programs."},{"key":"C","text":"It is plausible that less than 52% of the town''s population supports additional funding for community wellness programs."},{"key":"D","text":"It is plausible that more than 64% of the town''s population supports additional funding for community wellness programs."}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-04","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"}'::jsonb,'"B"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-04)."'::jsonb),
('0f9aea53-4b38-5471-92ca-8f93ce46dc7c'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"[Figure: CP-c26-05 — scatterplot with line of best fit; x 17–25, y 56–80; points near (17,64), (18,72), (19,68), (20,75), (21,66), (22,73), (23,65), (24,68), (25,77); line from ≈(17,67) to ≈(25,73)]\nThe scatterplot shows the relationship between two variables, x and y. A line of best fit for the data is also shown. At x = 21, which of the following is closest to the amount by which the y-value predicted by the line of best fit exceeds the actual y-value?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"1.5"},{"key":"B","text":"3"},{"key":"C","text":"4"},{"key":"D","text":"6"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-05","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/CP-c26-05.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for CP-c26-05","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"B"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-05)."'::jsonb),
('d3cd1fd4-c4d4-51bc-ae77-9aeea151ebf5'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"For a poll, an organizer randomly selected 21 professors from a certain liberal arts program at a university. These professors were asked whether they were interested in using a new textbook for their classes, and 14 of them responded \"Yes.\" What is the largest group to which the results of the poll can be generalized?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"The 14 professors who responded \"Yes\""},{"key":"B","text":"All liberal arts professors at the university"},{"key":"C","text":"All professors in the program"},{"key":"D","text":"All professors at the university"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-06","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"}'::jsonb,'"C"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-06)."'::jsonb),
('99f070ae-0ea6-5884-8ee5-9f6af5bdd6cd'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'medium','grid_in','"[Figure: CP-c26-07 — scatterplot of wingspan (inches, 7.0–10.5) vs weight (ounces, 0.4–2.0) for 13 sparrows]\nThe scatterplot shows the weight, in ounces, and the wingspan, in inches, for 13 sparrows. How many of these sparrows weigh less than 1.4 ounces and have a wingspan greater than 9.25 inches?"'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-07","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/CP-c26-07.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for CP-c26-07","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"4"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-07)."'::jsonb),
('0479371e-3aa8-5db9-a402-e6ab6ca5d91e'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"[Figure: CP-c26-08 — scatterplot with line of best fit; x 0–100, y 0–14; line from ≈(0,11.5) downward to ≈(100,6.5); points scattered around the line]\nIn the scatterplot, a line of best fit for the data is shown. Which of the following equations best represents this line of best fit?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"y = 11.5 − 0.05x"},{"key":"B","text":"y = 11.5 − 0.5x"},{"key":"C","text":"y = −11.5 + 0.05x"},{"key":"D","text":"y = −11.5 + 0.5x"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-08","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/CP-c26-08.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for CP-c26-08","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"A"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-08)."'::jsonb),
('9d58fe28-0484-5437-b4db-5f5905536e7f'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"[Figure: CP-c26-09 — scatterplot with line of best fit; x 0–7, y 0–7; points near (0,1), (1,3), (2,4.5), (3,6), (4,3), (5,4.5), (6,7), (7,6); line from ≈(0,2) to ≈(7,6.5)]\nIn the given scatterplot, a line of best fit for the data is shown. Which of the following is closest to the slope of the line of best fit?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"−1.56"},{"key":"B","text":"−0.64"},{"key":"C","text":"0.64"},{"key":"D","text":"1.56"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-09","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/CP-c26-09.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for CP-c26-09","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"C"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-09)."'::jsonb),
('4c93cf3b-4fc6-5ae2-8644-e555d9f61767'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"An economist conducted research to determine whether there is a relationship between the price of food and population density. He collected data from a random sample of 100 U.S. cities and found significant evidence that the price of food is lower in places with a high population density. Which of the following conclusions is best supported by these results?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"In U.S. cities, there is a positive association between the price of food and population density."},{"key":"B","text":"In U.S. cities, there is a negative association between the price of food and population density."},{"key":"C","text":"In U.S. cities, a decrease in the price of food is caused by an increase in the population density."},{"key":"D","text":"In U.S. cities, an increase in the population density is caused by a decrease in the price of food."}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-10","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"}'::jsonb,'"B"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-10)."'::jsonb),
('9fb1b723-b9ae-5558-b9ac-7ffa482b71fc'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'easy','mcq','"[Figure: CP-c26-11 — scatterplot; x 0–10, y 0–30; points near (0,24), (1,20), (2,17), (3,17), (4,9), (5,9), (6,3), (7,1); decreasing trend]\nThe scatterplot shows the relationship between x and y. Which of the following equations is the most appropriate linear model for the data shown?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"y = −0.7x − 24.2"},{"key":"B","text":"y = −0.7x + 24.2"},{"key":"C","text":"y = −3.4x − 24.2"},{"key":"D","text":"y = −3.4x + 24.2"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-11","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/CP-c26-11.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for CP-c26-11","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"D"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-11)."'::jsonb),
('e3fe0cda-70aa-5d8d-b5c3-972b6b97d340'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"[Table: CP-c26-12 — poll of 500 people: 0 times → 150, 1 time → 125, 2 times → 50, 3 times → 75, 4 or more times → 100]\nIn a poll, 500 people were chosen at random from a population of 36,000 and asked how many times they used public transportation in the last month. The table shows the results of the poll. Based on these results, which of the following is the best estimate of the total number of people in the population who used public transportation at least two times in the last month?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"3,600"},{"key":"B","text":"9,000"},{"key":"C","text":"16,200"},{"key":"D","text":"19,800"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-12","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/CP-c26-12.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for CP-c26-12","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"C"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-12)."'::jsonb),
('e1f53c6b-143a-55f9-96eb-5e66047a7a9d'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'easy','mcq','"A restaurant manager asked a random sample of 100 customers how long they waited for their order. Based on the responses, the manager estimated that the mean wait time for an order is 7 minutes, with an associated margin of error of 2 minutes. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion from this information?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"It is likely that most customers wait 7 minutes for their order."},{"key":"B","text":"It is likely that all customers wait between 5 and 9 minutes for their order."},{"key":"C","text":"It is plausible that the mean wait time for an order is between 5 and 9 minutes."},{"key":"D","text":"It is not possible that the mean wait time for an order is less than 5 minutes or greater than 9 minutes."}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-13","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"}'::jsonb,'"C"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-13)."'::jsonb),
('4bffa68d-ea7b-5336-bae2-fa83d783f804'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"A researcher conducted a study to determine if certain memorization strategies could improve the performance of chess players. For the study, the researcher selected a random sample of adults who are competing in a chess tournament. At the end of the study, 80% of those sampled reported that they did not find the memorization strategies to be useful during their chess games. Which of the following is the largest population to which the results of this study can be applied?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"All chess players who compete in tournaments"},{"key":"B","text":"All adults who compete in chess tournaments"},{"key":"C","text":"All chess players competing in the tournament"},{"key":"D","text":"All adults competing in the tournament"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-14","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"}'::jsonb,'"D"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-14)."'::jsonb),
('68dac1d2-5881-5caf-8324-3c2226c33cac'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'easy','mcq','"For a quality control test, a food manufacturing company selected a random sample of 100 granola bars from a large batch it produced. Based on the results of this test, it was estimated that the mean amount of fiber per bar is 5 grams, with an associated margin of 1.4 grams. The company then repeated the test with a random sample of 200 granola bars from the same batch. If the mean and margin of error were calculated the same way for both tests, which of the following statements is most likely true?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"The mean from the second test is smaller than the mean from the first test."},{"key":"B","text":"The mean from the second test is larger than the mean from the first test."},{"key":"C","text":"The margin of error from the second test is smaller than the margin of error from the first test."},{"key":"D","text":"The margin of error from the second test is larger than the margin of error from the first test."}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-15","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"}'::jsonb,'"C"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-15)."'::jsonb),
('7b82313c-b9a2-5fac-be19-c29993859954'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"Researchers must conduct an experiment to see whether a new vaccine is effective in relieving certain allergies. They have selected a random sample of 100 patients suffering from allergies. Some of the patients are assigned to the new vaccine while the rest are assigned to the traditional treatment. Which of the following methods of assigning each patient''s treatment is most likely to lead to a reliable conclusion about the effectiveness of the new vaccine?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"Those who have more than one allergy are assigned to the new vaccine."},{"key":"B","text":"The median weight of the patients is found. Those who weigh less than the median weight are assigned to the new vaccine."},{"key":"C","text":"The patients divide themselves evenly into two groups. A coin is tossed to decide which group is assigned to the new vaccine."},{"key":"D","text":"Each patient is given a random number. Those with an even number are assigned to the new vaccine. ### Exercise 2"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-16","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"}'::jsonb,'"D"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-16)."'::jsonb),
('a5888c95-f1e7-5bce-a90f-90c9cb67b63c'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"A city has a total of 75,300 voters. A journalist polled a random sample of 900 voters and found that 660 of those polled will vote for the current mayor in the next election. Based on this poll, what is the expected total number of city voters who will vote for the current mayor in the next election?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"20,080"},{"key":"B","text":"48,640"},{"key":"C","text":"55,220"},{"key":"D","text":"62,360"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-17","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"}'::jsonb,'"C"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-17)."'::jsonb),
('83f3f016-1bd1-5abf-9814-cc1c795c05f9'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"A group of school administrators wanted to gather opinions on new teacher certification requirements that were introduced in a certain state. The group surveyed a random sample of math teachers from one of the school districts in the state. The survey showed that the majority of the teachers in the sample support the new requirements. Which of the following is the largest population to which the results of the survey can be generalized?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"All math teachers in the school district"},{"key":"B","text":"All teachers in the school district"},{"key":"C","text":"All math teachers in the state"},{"key":"D","text":"The teachers in the sample"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-18","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"}'::jsonb,'"A"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-18)."'::jsonb),
('590bd050-4787-5994-a6cb-5f2dfe6e36c7'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'easy','mcq','"A nutritionist wants to conduct a survey to estimate the mean number of servings of vegetables eaten by a certain group of people each day. The nutritionist has not yet decided which group of people to survey. Surveying a random sample from which of the following groups would most likely result in the smallest margin of error?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"Residents of the same city"},{"key":"B","text":"Customers of a certain restaurant"},{"key":"C","text":"Viewers of the same television show"},{"key":"D","text":"Students who are following the same daily diet plan"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-19","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"}'::jsonb,'"D"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-19)."'::jsonb),
('afd0b7ae-4942-5e1b-b331-783eb339ff69'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'easy','mcq','"In a study, a biologist selected a random sample of 125 crabs from a local population and found that the mean carapace width of the crabs in the sample was 5.4 inches. Based on this information, which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. The mean carapace width of all the crabs in the local population is 5.4 inches.\nII. If another random sample of 125 crabs were selected from the local population, the mean carapace width of the crabs in the sample would be 5.4 inches."'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"I only"},{"key":"B","text":"II only"},{"key":"C","text":"Neither I nor II"},{"key":"D","text":"Both I and II"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-20","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"}'::jsonb,'"C"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-20)."'::jsonb),
('7fe1b7da-d07c-5061-a09b-bf0eddcc7aa0'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"[Figure: CP-c26-21 — scatterplot with line of best fit; x 0–8, y 0–16; points near (0,12), (1,10), (2,15), (3,12), (4,12), (5,10), (5,6), (6,12), (7,2), (8,4); line decreasing from ≈(0,14) to ≈(8,4)]\nThe scatterplot shows the relationship between two variables, x and y. A line of best fit is also shown. For how many of the data points is the actual y-value at least 2 greater than the y-value predicted by the line of best fit?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"1"},{"key":"B","text":"2"},{"key":"C","text":"3"},{"key":"D","text":"4"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-21","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/CP-c26-21.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for CP-c26-21","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"C"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-21)."'::jsonb),
('9b53e061-c2c9-5199-9984-60b42fb43818'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"In a psychological study, 600 college gymnasts were randomly selected and put in high-stakes competitions to determine whether they performed better under pressure. Based on the results of this study, it is estimated that 65% of all college gymnasts perform better under pressure, with an associated margin of error of 4%. Which of the following inferences can appropriately be drawn based on the given margin of error?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"The percentage of all college gymnasts who perform better under pressure is between 65% and 69%."},{"key":"B","text":"Approximately 4% of the college gymnasts in the study who performed better under pressure do not actually perform better under pressure."},{"key":"C","text":"It is unlikely that the percentage of all college gymnasts who perform better under pressure is 60%."},{"key":"D","text":"It is not possible that the percentage of all college gymnasts who perform better under pressure is 70%."}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-22","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"}'::jsonb,'"C"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-22)."'::jsonb),
('6778f260-c64c-5c8b-8f36-e11df3a48489'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"[Figure: CP-c26-23 — scatterplot with line of best fit; x 0–10, y 0–11; points near (4,0), (5,3), (6,3), (6,1), (6,8), (7,6), (7,11), (8,10); steep increasing line through ≈(4.3,0) and ≈(8,10.7)]\nThe scatterplot shows the relationship between two variables, x and y. A line of best fit is also shown. Which equation best represents the line of best fit shown?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"y = −2.9x + 12.4"},{"key":"B","text":"y = 2.9x + 12.4"},{"key":"C","text":"y = −2.9x − 12.4"},{"key":"D","text":"y = 2.9x − 12.4"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-23","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/CP-c26-23.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for CP-c26-23","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"D"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-23)."'::jsonb),
('54d0dbd7-909d-58f5-a707-f49f766db68d'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"Two candidates are running for governor of a state. A recent poll reports that out of a random sample of 250 voters, 110 support candidate A and 140 support candidate B. An estimated 500,000 state residents are expected to vote on election day. According to the poll, candidate B is expected to receive how many more votes than candidate A?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"60,000"},{"key":"B","text":"130,000"},{"key":"C","text":"220,000"},{"key":"D","text":"280,000"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-24","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"}'::jsonb,'"A"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-24)."'::jsonb),
('a6a37c65-f05b-5f24-854e-eb7942b19ef5'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"[Figure: CP-c26-25 — scatterplot with line of best fit; x 0–8, y 0–24; points near (0,21), (1,17), (2,10), (3,19), (3,11), (4,9), (5,14), (6,7), (7,9), (8,8); line decreasing from ≈(0,17.5) to ≈(8,7.5)]\nThe scatterplot shows the relationship between two variables, x and y. A line of best fit is also shown. Which of the following is closest to the slope of the line of best fit shown?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"−2.16"},{"key":"B","text":"−1.38"},{"key":"C","text":"−0.72"},{"key":"D","text":"−0.46"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-25","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/CP-c26-25.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for CP-c26-25","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"B"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-25)."'::jsonb),
('66ec1637-f9a1-5f5b-98e2-3a6786d1250b'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"All the residents of a town were mailed a survey about proposed renovations to the town''s high school. Approximately 10% of the residents completed the survey and mailed it back. Based on the responses, it was estimated that 72% of the residents support the proposed renovations, with an associated margin of error of 12%. Which of the following best explains why these survey results are unlikely to represent the opinions of all the town''s residents?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"The survey included residents who do not attend the high school."},{"key":"B","text":"Not all residents were aware of the proposed renovations before the survey."},{"key":"C","text":"Responses to the survey were not obtained from a random sample of the town''s residents."},{"key":"D","text":"The margin of error is greater than the percentage of residents who responded to the survey."}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-26","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"}'::jsonb,'"C"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-26)."'::jsonb),
('2d6f5208-ce47-5748-804c-a0af6d7d3c91'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"A company with 900 employees selected a random sample of 300 employees and asked each of them, \"Are you satisfied with the company''s training materials?\" Based on the sample, it is estimated that 82% of the company''s employees are satisfied with the company''s training materials, with an associated margin of error of 6%. Which of the following is the most plausible conclusion?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"Between 228 and 264 employees at the company are satisfied with the company''s training materials."},{"key":"B","text":"Between 684 and 792 employees at the company are satisfied with the company''s training materials."},{"key":"C","text":"Because the sample included employees who are not satisfied with the company''s training materials, no conclusion can be made."},{"key":"D","text":"Because the sample did not include all the employees who are satisfied with the company''s training materials, no conclusion can be made."}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-27","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"}'::jsonb,'"B"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-27)."'::jsonb),
('27a1f5f4-740e-5142-881d-dffb2ea8df22'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"[Figure: CP-c26-28 — scatterplot for data set A with line of best fit; x 0–8, y 0–12; points near (0,1), (1,5), (2,2.5), (2,7), (4,9), (5,7), (6,7.5), (6,10.5), (7,10), (8,12); line from ≈(0,2.3) to ≈(8,11.5)]\nThe scatterplot shows the relationship between two variables, x and y, for data set A. A line of best fit is shown. Data set B is created by adding 1.6 to the y-coordinate of each data point in data set A. Which of the following equations best represents a line of best fit for data set B?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"y = 2.4 + 1.2x"},{"key":"B","text":"y = 2.4 + 2.8x"},{"key":"C","text":"y = 4 + 1.2x"},{"key":"D","text":"y = 4 + 2.8x"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-28","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/CP-c26-28.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for CP-c26-28","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"C"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-28)."'::jsonb),
('1273589c-894b-58ed-b040-771ea720b23d'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"A researcher wanted to determine whether studying at a library is more effective than studying at home. He selected a random sample of 200 students and gave them three days to study for the same test. The 100 students who lived closest to a library were asked to study at a library, and the remaining 100 students were asked to study at home. After the students took the test, the scores of those who studied at a library were compared to the scores of those who studied at home. How should this experiment be changed so that the researcher can conclude whether studying at a library is more effective than studying at home?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"The students assigned to study at a library should study at the same library."},{"key":"B","text":"The students should be randomly assigned to study either at a library or at home."},{"key":"C","text":"The students assigned to study at a library should take a more difficult test than the students assigned to study at home."},{"key":"D","text":"No changes to the experiment are necessary."}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-29","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"}'::jsonb,'"B"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-29)."'::jsonb),
('e01d58e4-976a-54f7-a5bd-0c76b256beb0'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"A company consists of full-time and part-time employees. A sample of 60 employees was selected at random from the company and given a survey to determine how many social events the company should have each year. There are 59 part-time employees and 1 full-time employee in the sample. Which of the following is the largest population to which the results of the survey can be generalized?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"All part-time employees at the company"},{"key":"B","text":"All employees at the company"},{"key":"C","text":"The 59 part-time employees in the sample"},{"key":"D","text":"The 60 employees in the sample"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-30","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"}'::jsonb,'"B"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-30)."'::jsonb),
('28ce5748-3374-560c-97ec-9648fc25d121'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"[Table: CP-c26-31 — two random samples of a pet supply store''s customers: Sample A (27% own a cat, margin of error 4%); Sample B (30%, 6%)]\nFor a study, a pet supply store selected two random samples of its customers. Based on data gathered from each sample, the percentage of customers who own a cat was estimated. The results are shown in the table. If the margin of error was calculated the same way for both samples, which of the following is the most likely reason that the margin of error for sample B is greater than the margin of error for sample A?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"Sample B included a greater number of customers who own a cat than sample A."},{"key":"B","text":"Sample B included a greater percentage of customers who own a cat than sample A."},{"key":"C","text":"Sample B included more customers than sample A."},{"key":"D","text":"Sample B included fewer customers than sample A."}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-31","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/CP-c26-31.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for CP-c26-31","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"D"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-31)."'::jsonb),
('a1fefd30-d9dc-56f6-b280-635a7311ec6e'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'easy','mcq','"A residential building has 15 floors. To estimate the mean age of all the residents who live in the building, Rami randomly selected 3 floors and surveyed the 102 residents who live on those floors. The mean age of the residents surveyed is 31 years. Which of the following statements must be true?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"The mean age of all the residents who live in the building is 31 years."},{"key":"B","text":"It is plausible that the mean age of all the residents who live in the building is 31 years."},{"key":"C","text":"The mean age of all the residents who live in the building should not be estimated because the sample size is too small."},{"key":"D","text":"The mean age of all the residents who live in the building should not be estimated because the sampling method is flawed. ---"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"panda.md","source_sha256":"e120378a8127836a41962876a763889bbb1df92d70c90242206130acfa43e008","source_question_id":"CP-c26-32","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"}'::jsonb,'"D"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from panda.md (CP-c26-32)."'::jsonb),
